  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain associated with the remnants of Hurricane Isaias \narrives over the weekend.\n\nHigh pressure will keep dry and warm conditions today, only\nseeing increasing cloud cover throughout the day in advance of \nIsaias. Will see highs above normal again..low to mid 70s by the\nafternoon.\n\nHigh pressure will begin to slide east by Saturday as the\nremnants of Isaias approach the forecast area from the southwest.\nThis high pressure will wedge south east of the Appalachians as\nrain moves into the region, keeping conditions on the cooler \nside for Saturday and into a portion of Sunday. Rain should \nbegin across North Carolina sometime late Saturday morning and \nthen overspread the forecast areas throughout the day. Rain will\ncontinue into Sunday morning, becoming a bit more scattered by \nSunday afternoon. Overall, this appears to be a widespread 1 to \n2 inches, with lowest totals west of the Blue Ridge given \nsomewhat of a rain shadow effect.\n\nFavored regions for higher totals look to be along portions of \nthe Blue Ridge as a 50-60kt southeasterly 850mb LLJ pushes into\nthe area as the remnants of Isaias move into the Tennessee \nvalley. Upslope flow from this LLJ will likely lead to increased\nrainfall totals into the 2 to 4 inches range, with perhaps some\nlocalized higher amounts particularly in northwest North\nCarolina. This general area has the greatest concern for\nflooding given the upslope enhancement, along with increasing\ndeep tropical moisture (PWAT values nearing 2\"), which could\nresult in periods of higher intensity rain rates. Timeframe for\nthe highest rainfall rates based on REFS guidance will be late\nSaturday night into early Sunday morning. Flood Watches may be\nwarranted in this area if trends continue to show the higher\nrainfall potential.\n\nThe aforementioned 850mb LLJ may lead to a downslope wind event\nfor areas in SE West Virginia and SW Virginia. Right now, \nforecast wind gusts are just below Wind Advisory criteria; \nhowever, as high res CAM guidance comes into range, it is \npossible that these forecasted wind gusts may increase across \nmountain locations even as whats left of Isaias continues to \ndissipate as it moves near the region. \n\nAlso, still watching trends Sunday afternoon with models now\nhinting at high pressure retreating enough to allow for a brief\nperiod of destabilization along a triple point in Central\nVirginia, which could pose a brief tornado threat. Strong \nTheta-E advection on the RRFS/NAM guidance shows this potential \nrather well. Will not take much destablization to support\nrotating low-topped supercells given 0-1km SRH exceeding \n200m2s2 and Bulk Shear 40-50kts.\n\nBy late Monday, all remnants of Isaias look to be well east of\nthe region, with dry conditions returning areawide once again. A\npossible dry frontal passage by the Tuesday night timeframe\nlooks to drop temperatures slightly through the middle of the\nwork week. Overall, the region will likely not see any rain\nchances through most of next week after the remnants from \nIsaias move through over the weekend.",
